@prefix sh:        <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs:      <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix orexis:        <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix progression: <http://example.org/orexis/progression#> .
@prefix sosa:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix market:    <http://example.org/orexis/market#> .
@prefix actuation: <http://example.org/orexis/actuation#> .
@prefix ssn:        <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix ssn-system: <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/systems/> .
@prefix sensing:    <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .
@prefix xsd:        <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#  THE ACTIONS THIS PACKAGE BRINGS — one node each, and the whole of one (knowledge/domain/action.md):
#    orexis:available  its PRECONDITION — a SELECT over the world binding ?property ?via ?direction,
#                  and ?for_agent where the row is a duty owed to someone; `$me` and
#                  `$properties` substituted by the collector, prefixes prepended by the store;
#    sh:construct  its EFFECT, with orexis:retracts, orexis:landsAfter and orexis:confirmedBy beside it;
#    (who carries it out is the code's to say: `@contributes(<this action>)` on its module.)
#  Precondition, effect and taker were three files and a fourth surface; a STRIPS operator is one
#  thing, and an author adds a way of acting by adding a node here and a `take()` to a module.

#################  What BUYING would make true  #################
#
#  The lever every plant in this project actually pulls, and the planner could not see it. Only
#  sensing and actuation shipped effects, so a search over a plant's menu saw Observe alone,
#  correctly found that looking does not wet soil, marked the plan PARTIAL and deferred to the
#  reflex — which is what waters the society. Measured the hour #265 landed: `better` was zero
#  across three societies and `blind` was 1 for every plant. The planner decided correctly and
#  could not see the one lever that mattered. See #268.
#
#  THE HONEST DIFFICULTY, stated rather than smuggled: **an agent does not control whether it
#  WINS.** A dose is an act whose effect follows from taking it; a bid is an act whose effect
#  follows from taking it AND from a host matching it AND from nobody outbidding it. So this
#  rule predicts the world in which the bid clears, which is optimistic, and the optimism is
#  the correct choice for three reasons rather than a convenience:
#
#    * It is what planning does everywhere. A STRIPS action schema states what the action
#      achieves, not what it achieves times a probability; uncertainty lives in re-planning and
#      in monitoring, which is where this repo already puts it.
#    * The machinery for the unmet case already exists and already runs. An Acquire that does
#      not clear produces an UNMET verdict through the same expectation path a dose that never
#      landed does — see `an-intention-is-an-amortised-deliberation` on the verification arc.
#      A rule that hedged here would be inventing a second, quieter way to be disappointed.
#    * A pessimistic rule is not more honest, it is just useless: predicting no change would
#      make Acquire indistinguishable from doing nothing, and the search would go on refusing
#      to bid — which is exactly the bug this closes.
#
#  What it must NOT do is hide that. The optimism is why `orexis:landsAfter` below is generous and
#  why `orexis:confirmedBy` is by OBSERVATION: nothing is true until a reading says so.

market:Acquiring
    a orexis:Action ;

    #  THE METHOD (#523): Acquiring is the DECISION to enter this venue at a price, and the
    #  search chooses it by its effect and its cost. What taking it comes to is two steps the
    #  keeper walks on feedback — tender a bid into the open round, then present the claim
    #  it wins — each saying what it waits for below. Losing is Tendering lapsing at the
    #  round's close with no claim held: the tail is dropped and deliberation is asked again,
    #  which is the next round. The protocol used to be a chain of reactions in bidding.py.
    orexis:method ( market:Tendering market:Presenting ) ;

    #  AVAILABLE while a ROUND IS OPEN in a market I bid in WHOSE WINNINGS CAN PHYSICALLY REACH
    #  MY SUBJECT: the host
    #  holds an actuator plumbed to my own pot, so opening it puts the good where I am. Deduced
    #  link by link; only the last — that the good RAISES this property — is stated, once, by
    #  the domain, because it is physics. The valuation joins through the venue's good (#198):
    #  property alone put both directions on both rows the day two venues priced one property.
    orexis:available """
        SELECT DISTINCT ?want ?about ?via ?direction WHERE {
          VALUES (?want ?about) { $wants }
          $me orexis:actsFor ?subject .
          $me market:bidsIn ?via .
          ?via market:marketFor ?src .
          ?src market:supplies ?good .
          ?host market:hosts ?via ;
                actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
          ?lever actuation:actuates ?subject .
          ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ;
                     market:aboutProperty ?about ;
                     market:direction ?direction .
          #  AND A ROUND IS OPEN (#358). The hop the walk was missing: without it the row said
          #  "I could buy here", and the keeper committed an Acquire that then waited for the
          #  market to knock. The round is what THIS agent was told, in its own graph, so the
          #  hop names $beliefs.
          #
          #  THE ROW'S PRESENCE IS THE OPENNESS (#599). This asked `?closes > NOW()` because a
          #  bidder that lost was told nothing and had to end the round by its own arithmetic.
          #  The host declares the close now, on the topic that carried the offer, and the row
          #  is retracted when it arrives — so what is left to ask is whether the row is there.
          #  A rule reading a triple asks no clock, in a simulated world or a real one, which
          #  is what a WHERE asking NOW() could never do: the engine answers the real now, not
          #  the instant the act would be taken at (#598).
          GRAPH $beliefs { ?via market:hasRound ?round . ?round market:closesAt ?closes }
        }""" ;

    #  WHAT IT SPENDS — the landing's twin (#466): the bidder's own CEILING, litres times
    #  the pick it would never bid above. An estimate, and declared as one: the clearing
    #  price is unknowable before the round (and under uniform price usually lower), so the
    #  package declares the bound it owns rather than the guess it does not. The water term
    #  is the same one deliberately domain-coupled corner `beliefs.py` argues for
    #  `maxValuePerL`, speaking here for the same reason: what a bid is WORTH is the
    #  domain's to say.
    orexis:costs """
        SELECT ?cost WHERE {
            $me market:bidsIn ?via .
            ?via market:marketFor ?src .
            ?src market:supplies ?good .
            ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ;
                       market:aboutProperty $about .
            GRAPH $beliefs { $me <http://example.org/orexis/water#maxValuePerL> ?ceiling }
            #  WHAT ONE ACT SPENDS, at the ceiling this agent set (#579). It was the ceiling
            #  times the litres the search had sized, and the search sizes nothing now: a lot
            #  costs what a lot costs, and how many litres are bought is settled when the
            #  bidder tenders. What the ranking needs of this is unchanged — a bought lot is
            #  dearer than a dose from a vessel already held, which is why a plant with a
            #  valve doses rather than buys.
            BIND(?ceiling AS ?cost)
        }""" ;

    rdfs:label "what winning a bid makes true" ;

    #  THE SAME EFFECT AS A DOSE, and that is the point rather than a coincidence. Water
    #  bought and water poured bring the same property into its region; what differs is who
    #  owns the valve and whether anyone had to be paid. So the prediction is the same band a
    #  dose reaches (#579) — reached along the plumbing walk `affordances.rq` already deduces
    #  the row from: a venue I bid in, whose source supplies a good, whose host holds a lever
    #  plumbed to MY subject. An agent bidding in a market no valve connects to its pot gets
    #  no row and no effect, because a delivery that cannot reach you is, for you, no lever
    #  at all. The conversion is walked so the rule states its premise — a valuation this
    #  agent holds — and read for its number by the bidder when it tenders.
    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?obs a sosa:Observation , ?band ;
                 sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                 sosa:observedProperty $about ;
                 sosa:resultTime ?now .
        } WHERE {
            $me orexis:actsFor $subject ; market:bidsIn ?via .
            ?via market:marketFor ?source .
            ?source market:supplies ?good .
            ?host market:hosts ?via ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:actuates $subject .
            ?term market:ofGood ?good ; market:aboutProperty $about .
            GRAPH $beliefs { $me ?term ?conversion }
            FILTER(?conversion > 0)
            #  THE BAND A BOUGHT LOT REACHES: in the region, of this subject's property — the member genesis
            #  minted from the range the subject states — from below it or from inside it.
            #  From ABOVE it, more water helps nothing: the reading stays in the family it is
            #  in, which is the same world as before and pruned as one. Where the property
            #  stands is read by this rule from the world it is asked about, so the step's
            #  precondition carries it — the band the reading is in, never a number; optional,
            #  since a lot may be planned before any reading exists.
            #  The wetness test sits INSIDE the standing reading's pattern: outside it, an
            #  unbound ?standing would join freely with any reading above its region.
            #  A READING MUST STAND, and the requirement is load-bearing rather than
            #  defensive. Where the property has never been read, this rule yielding a
            #  reading would FABRICATE one — and a world that knows where the pot is scores
            #  better than one that does not, so a content plant would buy water to find out
            #  how wet it is (#137, the guard the zero-size filter used to carry). Requiring
            #  the reading leaves such a world untouched and the step discarded as somewhere
            #  already seen, which is the road every other "this does not help" arrives by;
            #  a look comes first, as it always did.
            GRAPH $state { ?standing sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                             sosa:observedProperty $about .
                           OPTIONAL { ?standing a ?tooWet .
                                      FILTER(?tooWet = sensing:AboveRegion) } }
            BIND(IF(BOUND(?tooWet), sensing:AboveRegion, sensing:InRegion) AS ?family)
            ?band rdfs:subClassOf ?family ;
                  sensing:ofSubject $subject ; sensing:ofProperty $about .
            BIND(BNODE() AS ?obs)
            #  STAMPED WHEN THIS ACT'S CHANGE COMPLETES (#588), not when the plan was made:
            #  a reading this construct predicts exists at the instant the act lands, and
            #  `$lands` is that instant — the node's own plus this act's `orexis:landsAfter`.
            BIND($lands AS ?now)
        }""" ;

    #  WHEN IT LANDS — and this is where buying and dosing genuinely differ, so the number is
    #  built from what a BIDDER can see rather than from what a host knows.
    #
    #  Two legs are visible and one is not. `market:redeemWindowS` is on the VENUE, so a bidder
    #  reads it: it is how long a winner has to present, and using the whole window rather than
    #  a guess at how promptly this agent will present is deliberate — an upper bound is the
    #  safe direction here, because patience shorter than an effect's landing time lets an agent
    #  act before it could know (#250). The host's valve then pours, and that leg is the
    #  identical arithmetic `actuation:ActuateEffect` uses, off the same public device facts.
    #
    #  WHAT IS EXCLUDED, and cannot honestly be included: the time until a round opens and
    #  closes. `market:bidWindowS` and `market:roundCooldownS` are the HOST's private beliefs —
    #  a bidder is not told them, and must not be, because a venue that published its schedule
    #  would let a bidder time its arrival. So this is the landing time of a bid that finds a
    #  round already open. A bid that has to wait for one lands later than this says, and the
    #  honest name for that gap is the convening seam
    #  (`the-lot-is-the-hosts-standing-offer`), not a number to invent here.
    orexis:landsAfter """
        SELECT ?seconds WHERE {
            $me market:bidsIn ?via .
            ?via market:redeemWindowS ?window ; market:marketFor ?source .
            ?host market:hosts ?via ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:actuates $subject ;
                   actuation:maxDoseMl ?cap ;
                   actuation:mlPerSecond ?rate .
            FILTER(?rate > 0)
            BIND(IF($litres * 1000 < ?cap, $litres * 1000, ?cap) AS ?ml)
            BIND(?window + (?ml / ?rate) AS ?seconds)
        }""" ;

    #  BY OBSERVATION, and for a sharper reason than the dose's. A claim is co-signed and a
    #  valve reports that it opened, so it is tempting to call this confirmed by report. But a
    #  report says the SUPPLIER acted; only a reading says the water reached this pot. That gap
    #  is exactly `an-unconfirmed-dose-is-not-a-delivered-one` seen from the buyer's side, and
    #  the buyer is the one who paid.

    #  The reading it replaces, identical to the dose's and for the identical reason: the sensed
    #  graph holds one node per (subject, property), so a predicted reading left beside the old
    #  one would put two results on one node and a reader would take whichever it found first.
    #  Retracts NOTHING when it adds nothing, for the same reason and by the same guard: a
    #  zero-size act that removed the standing reading would leave the world blinder than it
    #  found it, which is the opposite of the error above and just as wrong.
    #  The reading it replaces, unconditionally (#579). It was gated on `$litres > 0`, so a
    #  bid sized to nothing left the standing reading alone and changed no world; there is no
    #  size here now, and the gate is the required standing reading in the construct — with
    #  the filter left in place and litres bound at nothing, NOTHING was retracted and the
    #  world came back holding both readings, the old one still below the region.
    orexis:retracts """
        CONSTRUCT { ?obs ?p ?o } WHERE {
            GRAPH $state {
                ?obs sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                     sosa:observedProperty $about ;
                     ?p ?o
            }
        }""" .

#################  What OFFERING makes true  #################
#
#  The host's move, as an ACTION at last (a-round-is-a-fact-and-offering-is-an-action). It
#  was a handler: hear a LOW, check a cooldown and a dict, announce; and when the vessel was
#  dry, remember the LOW in a second dict and announce on the next reading. Every one of those
#  checks is a PRECONDITION and belongs on the node — so "acquire upstream, then offer" is a
#  two-step the search finds from Acquiring's effect (stock rises) and this row's premise
#  (stock is there), two nodes that never mention each other.
#
#  What is NOT decided here is whether a host would RATHER sell: the want this action serves
#  is a call — a participant's LOW made a round wanted — and the strategic-supplier seam is
#  untouched.

market:Offering
    a orexis:Action ;

    #  AVAILABLE on a venue I host, while NO round stands on it (in what I hold, or in the
    #  world a plan is imagining — an Offer already taken in this plan leaves no room for a
    #  second), my cooldown has run out (an instant I wrote at the last close; the duration
    #  itself is private and never on a row), and the vessel holds SOMETHING by my own
    #  witness — or I have no witness at all, which is the mains, and sells as it always did.
    #  The lot is min(offer, stock), as `announce` always sized it: a partial lot is a real
    #  round (a-round-is-sized-by-the-vessel), so the premise is stock > 0 and not stock >= lot.
    #
    #  ?property is the stock I would be watching, or the good's own property where I watch
    #  nothing — the row needs a name and the intention is keyed by it; no direction, because
    #  offering moves no water. The serve does.
    orexis:available """
        SELECT DISTINCT ?about ?via WHERE {
          $me market:hosts ?via .
          ?via market:marketFor ?source .
          ?source market:supplies ?good .
          ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ; market:aboutProperty ?goodProperty .
          OPTIONAL { $me sensing:polls ?witness .
                     ?witness sensing:monitors ?source ; sosa:observes ?stock }
          BIND(COALESCE(?stock, ?goodProperty) AS ?about)
          #  AND NO ROUND OF MINE IS OPEN — the row again, and the host retracts its own at
          #  close, so there is nothing to compare (#599).
          FILTER NOT EXISTS {
            GRAPH $beliefs { ?via market:hasRound ?open } }
          FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH $state { ?via market:hasRound ?imagined } }
          #  AND THE VENUE IS NOT COOLING (#598): the row's presence is the fact, retracted
          #  by a timer when the cooldown runs out and by a sweep where the timer did not
          #  survive a restart. It asked `?may <= NOW()` while the term named the instant
          #  rather than the state, which is the last clock this package's rules read.
          FILTER NOT EXISTS { GRAPH $beliefs { ?via market:coolingUntil ?until } }
          OPTIONAL { GRAPH $state { ?reading sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ?source ;
                                    sosa:observedProperty ?stock ;
                                    sosa:hasSimpleResult ?level } }
          FILTER(!BOUND(?stock) || !BOUND(?level) || ?level > 0)
        }""" ;

    #  WHO CARRIES IT OUT — the host: it announces, exactly as it always did.
    rdfs:label "what opening a round makes true" ;

    #  THE EFFECT: a round on the venue — the same fact `announce` writes, predicted. It
    #  lands in the plan's own graph ($state, where every effect's triples go), which is why
    #  the precondition above looks there too and why a call's measure reads both graphs.
    #  The stock is untouched: offering moves no water. The round's close is `$lands` plus my
    #  bid window, in the duration arithmetic this engine does support — measured before it was
    #  leaned on — and `$lands` rather than NOW() since #588, an offering landing at once.
    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?via market:hasRound ?round .
            ?round a market:Round ;
                   market:roundId "imagined" ;
                   market:lotL ?lot ;
                   market:reservePerL ?reserve ;
                   market:closesAt ?closes .
        } WHERE {
            $me market:hosts ?via .
            GRAPH $beliefs { $me market:offerQuantityL ?lot ;
                                 market:reservePricePerL ?reserve ;
                                 market:bidWindowS ?window }
            BIND(BNODE() AS ?round)
            #  FROM WHEN THE ROUND OPENS (#588), which is when this act's change completes —
            #  at once, by construction, and `$lands` says so rather than the wall clock a
            #  pass happens to be running at.
            BIND($lands + xsd:dayTimeDuration(CONCAT("PT", STR(?window), "S")) AS ?closes)
        }""" ;

    #  AT ONCE, and BY CONSTRUCTION: saying so makes it so. The first effect here confirmed
    #  that way, and the reason the route was declared — nothing is waited for, nothing is
    #  read back; the round exists because the host announced it.
    orexis:landsAfter """
        SELECT (0 AS ?seconds) WHERE { }""" ;
    .


#################  Presenting a held claim  #################
#
#  The buyer's own act with a won claim: hold it until the watch is live, then present it on
#  the redeem channel. No precondition and no effect here, because no plan chooses it — the
#  claim arriving IS the adoption, and the watch going live is the trigger (#132). It is a
#  node so the hold can be a commitment (`progression:by market:Presenting`) and an urgency (a held
#  claim tightens the cadence), now that an intention commits to an action and nothing else.

#################  The steps Acquiring comes to  #################
#
#  Neither is on a menu: no precondition, no effect, adopted only by the method above. Each
#  says what it waits for, as a SELECT template with the rule's tokens, and the keeper holds
#  the step on it (`orexis:readyWhen` before it is taken, `orexis:doneWhen` after) and reads
#  when the wait is over off `orexis:lapsesAt`.

market:Tendering
    a orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "tendering a bid into the open round" ;
    #  DONE when a claim on this venue cleared to me since this intention was adopted —
    #  never a claim left over from an earlier round.
    orexis:doneWhen """
        SELECT $this WHERE {
          GRAPH $beliefs { $me market:holdsClaim ?claim .
                           ?claim market:onVenue $via ; market:claimedAt ?at . FILTER(?at > $since) } }""" ;
    #  ... or not by the time the round closes: a bidder is told the outcome or nothing.
    orexis:lapsesAt """
        SELECT (MAX(?t) AS ?at) WHERE {
          GRAPH $beliefs { $via market:hasRound ?round . ?round market:closesAt ?t } }""" .

market:Presenting
    a orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "presenting a held claim" ;
    #  READY when my watch on the property is provably live (#132, #135): the sensor
    #  monitoring my subject for what the lot moves says so, in sensing's words — or when
    #  the host already redeemed the claim for me (a venue with no redeem channel), and
    #  there is nothing to present but the watch to open.
    orexis:readyWhen """
        SELECT $this WHERE {
          { ?sensor sensing:monitors $subject ; sosa:observes $about ; sensing:watchLive true }
          UNION
          { GRAPH $beliefs { $me market:holdsClaim ?claim . ?claim market:onVenue $via ;
                             market:claimedAt ?at ; market:presentedAt ?done . FILTER(?at > $since) } } }""" ;
    #  ... and no longer than the sensor's own horizon: a dose delayed forever is worse than a
    #  dose unobserved, so the step lapses into being taken.
    orexis:lapsesAt """
        SELECT (MIN(?s) AS ?seconds) WHERE {
          ?sensor sensing:monitors $subject ; sosa:observes $about ; sensing:staleAfterS ?s }""" .


#################  What SERVING a presented claim makes true  #################
#
#  The rule #255 was missing, and the mirror of Acquire's: a host that HONOURS discharges the
#  debt and drains its vessel by the amount the record says it owes. The PREMISE is the vessel
#  holding enough — `?level >= ?litres` — and a premise that does not hold binds NOTHING, so
#  serving from a dry vessel predicts no change, the world keeps its parent's signature, and
#  the search discards the step as somewhere already reached. That silence is the whole
#  mechanism: the step that IS reachable from there is Acquire, whose effect raises the very
#  level this premise reads — so "refill, then serve" emerges as a two-step plan from two
#  rules that never mention each other, which is what a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs promised
#  chaining would be.
#
#  THE AMOUNT HAS ONE SOURCE: the obligation record, written when the claim was issued
#  (`orexis:amountL`). An obligation recorded before the amount existed simply never binds here —
#  unplannable, still servable by the honoured row, which is the graceful arrival every
#  widening in this repo is held to.

market:Serving
    a orexis:Action ;

    #  AVAILABLE as a DUTY — ?for_agent bound — for each buyer this venue admits who acts for a
    #  subject my valve serves: the ACL's shape applied to obligation, so a presentation from
    #  anyone else is a forgery before it is a duty. The valuation is tied by the RECIPIENT's
    #  stated need, not the good alone (#198 from the other side): a valve serving a pot must not
    #  also claim to move that pot's stored litres. Disclosure, not a filter — what an obligation
    #  makes me do arrives as a want, and the deliberator ranges over it like any other.
    orexis:available """
        SELECT ?about ?via ?direction ?for_agent WHERE {
          $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?via .
          ?venue market:marketFor ?source .
          ?via actuation:drawsFrom ?source ;
               actuation:actuates ?subject .
          ?source market:supplies ?good .
          ?for_agent market:bidsIn ?venue ; orexis:actsFor ?subject .
          ?subject ssn-system:hasOperatingRange/ssn-system:inCondition/ssn:forProperty ?about .
          ?valuation market:ofGood ?good ;
                     market:aboutProperty ?about ;
                     market:direction ?direction .
        }""" ;

    rdfs:label "what serving a presented claim makes true" ;

    #  WHAT IT MAKES TRUE is the DISCHARGE, and nothing about the level (#579). It used to
    #  predict the level a serve leaves — `?level - ?litres`, a number — and to fire only where
    #  the number covered the claim. Neither survives the ruling: an imagined world states what
    #  a reading IS, so there is no number in it to subtract from, and a rule reading one after
    #  another rule had written a band bound nothing at all, which broke the refill chain
    #  silently — acquire then serve, the one chain this domain has.
    #
    #  Both are restated in the domain's own words. WHETHER the vessel can serve is whether its
    #  level sits in the region the vessel states — the operating range keeps a litre in reserve,
    #  which is exactly "enough to sell" — so a barrel below its region serves nothing and the
    #  host must refill first. WHAT the serve does to the level is left unsaid: the water leaves,
    #  and the next reading says which band it lands in. Saying less is the honest declaration,
    #  and it is what lets the level's own want notice the drop.
    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?obl orexis:dischargedAt ?now .
        } WHERE {
            ?obl orexis:forClaim $claim ; orexis:amountL ?litres .
            FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?obl orexis:dischargedAt ?done }
            $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:drawsFrom ?vessel .
            ?vessel ssn-system:hasOperatingRange/ssn-system:inCondition/ssn:forProperty ?levelProp .
            GRAPH $state { ?cur sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest ?vessel ;
                                 sosa:observedProperty ?levelProp ;
                                 a sensing:InRegion }
            #  STAMPED WHEN THIS ACT'S CHANGE COMPLETES (#588), not when the plan was made:
            #  a reading this construct predicts exists at the instant the act lands, and
            #  `$lands` is that instant — the node's own plus this act's `orexis:landsAfter`.
            BIND($lands AS ?now)
        }""" ;

    #  WHEN IT LANDS: the valve's own open-seconds for the recorded amount, the identical
    #  arithmetic the dose and the bid state, off the same public device facts — a deadline
    #  computed anywhere else would be a second claim about when the world should answer.
    orexis:landsAfter """
        SELECT ?seconds WHERE {
            ?obl orexis:forClaim $claim ; orexis:amountL ?litres .
            $me market:hosts ?venue ; actuation:hasActuator ?lever .
            ?lever actuation:maxDoseMl ?cap ;
                   actuation:mlPerSecond ?rate .
            FILTER(?rate > 0)
            BIND(IF(?litres * 1000 < ?cap, ?litres * 1000, ?cap) AS ?ml)
            BIND(?ml / ?rate AS ?seconds)
        }""" .

    #  BY OBSERVATION, from the host's own side: the valve reports that it opened, but what
    #  this rule PREDICTS is the vessel's level, and only the next reading of it says the
    #  water left. The buyer's side confirms itself on the buyer's own arc, exactly as
    #  AcquireEffect argues from the other end of the same pipe.

    #  NOTHING IS RETRACTED (#579). It used to take the vessel's standing reading out, because
    #  the construct wrote a new one in its place; the construct predicts no reading now, and
    #  retracting the standing one would leave the world knowing nothing about the level —
    #  which reads as maximal ignorance and would make serving look like a way to forget.
